---
import ModuleLayout from '../../layouts/ModuleLayout.astro';
import modules from '../../data/modules.json';
import '../../styles/modules/religions.css';
import '../../styles/icons/religions.css';

const mod = modules.find((m) => m.id === 45)!;
---

<ModuleLayout mod={mod}>

  

    <div class="module-header">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-place-of-worship religions-text"></i>
      <h1 class="module-title module-heading">Module 45: Religions</h1>
    </div>
    <p class="module-subtitle">What religions are, why humans have always had them, and a guided tour of the major traditions</p>

    <!-- PART A -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-a-what-religion-is-the-phenomenon-and-its-study">Part A · what religion is — the phenomenon and its study</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Defining religion — a harder problem than it seems</p>
      <p class="cnote">Every definition of religion either includes things we would rather exclude (football fandom as "religion"?) or excludes things we would rather include (is Theravada Buddhism a religion if it has no god?). Scholars distinguish <em>substantive</em> definitions — religion involves belief in the supernatural — from <em>functional</em> ones: religion does what religion does, binding communities, answering ultimate questions, and marking the sacred. Neither works cleanly. Anthropologist Clifford Geertz proposed that a religion is "a system of symbols which acts to establish powerful, pervasive, and long-lasting moods and motivations." That captures something real, but it would also describe Maoism. The messiness is not a flaw; it reflects the fact that "religion" is a Western category that may not carve nature at its joints. Many traditions — Shinto, Confucianism, many Indigenous practices — do not have a word for "religion" at all.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Why humans are religious — evolutionary and psychological explanations</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-md">Cognitive science of religion (CSR) asks not whether any religion is true but why religion is universal. Four mechanisms dominate the literature:</p>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="csr-btns">
        <button onclick="showCSR('agent',this)" class="active">Agent detection</button>
        <button onclick="showCSR('coalition',this)">Coalitional psychology</button>
        <button onclick="showCSR('tmt',this)">Terror management</button>
        <button onclick="showCSR('boyer',this)">Boyer's minimally counter-intuitive</button>
      </div>
      <div class="explorer-panel" id="csr-panel"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">The numbers — who believes what and where (2024 estimates)</p>
      <div class="dist-bar u-mt-md" id="u-mt-10px"></div>
      <div class="dist-legend" id="religion-legend"></div>
      <p class="cnote u-mt-sm">Christianity is largest by count (2.4B, 31%) but Islam is fastest-growing: demographic projections suggest Islam will roughly match Christianity by 2070 due to younger age profiles in Muslim-majority countries. The "non-religious" 1.2B is heavily concentrated in China (where many identify as non-religious yet practice ancestor veneration) and Western Europe.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Religious studies as a discipline</p>
      <p class="cnote">Religious studies emerged in the 19th century as scholars tried to study religion empirically and comparatively, without either defending or attacking any tradition. Emile Durkheim (1912) argued religion is essentially social: the "sacred" marks off what a community holds collectively important, and ritual reinforces group cohesion. Max Weber's <em>Protestant Ethic</em> (1905) traced a line from Calvinist theology to capitalist discipline. William James (1902) took phenomenology seriously, asking what religious experience actually feels like from the inside. These three still define the main approaches: structuralist-sociological (Durkheim), historical-comparative (Weber), and experiential-phenomenological (James). Modern scholars add cognitive science and neuroscience, giving us perhaps the best tools yet for understanding religion without either worshipping or dismissing it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Sacred texts — how scriptures work and how they're interpreted</p>
      <p class="cnote">Most religions have texts they treat as authoritative, but "authoritative" means different things. For Muslims, the Quran is literally the word of God dictated to Muhammad — every Arabic word matters. For Jews, the Torah is also revealed, but the Talmud (rabbinic commentary developed over centuries) has equal practical authority in most denominations. For Christians, the Bible is "inspired" — but exactly how that word is interpreted separates the fundamentalist who reads Genesis literally from the liberal Protestant who treats it as theological poetry. Hinduism has no single canon at all: the Vedas, Upanishads, Bhagavad Gita, Puranas, and regional texts all claim varying degrees of authority. When someone says "the religion teaches X," always ask: which interpretation, by whom, in which tradition?</p>
    </div>

    <!-- PART B -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-b-the-abrahamic-religions-the-largest-family">Part B · the Abrahamic religions — the largest family</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Judaism — the oldest Abrahamic tradition</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-md">Judaism is the parent tradition of both Christianity and Islam — simultaneously a religion, an ethnicity, and a civilisation. About 15.7 million Jews exist worldwide (0.2% of humanity), yet Jewish thought has shaped Western law, ethics, and theology far beyond that proportion. Click a key concept:</p>
      <div class="elem-grid" id="judaism-grid"></div>
      <div class="elem-detail" id="judaism-detail"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Christianity — the world's largest religion and its diversity</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-md">With 2.4 billion adherents, Christianity is so internally varied that the label covers people who have almost nothing in common theologically. The core claim — that Jesus of Nazareth was the incarnate Son of God, died for human sin, and rose from the dead — is shared. Everything else diverges. Explore the main branches:</p>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="christianity-btns">
        <button onclick="showBranch('christianity','catholic',this)" class="active">Catholic</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('christianity','protestant',this)">Protestant</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('christianity','orthodox',this)">Eastern Orthodox</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('christianity','evangelical',this)">Evangelical</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('christianity','liberation',this)">Liberation theology</button>
      </div>
      <div class="branch-panel" id="christianity-panel"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Islam — the fastest-growing religion</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-md">Islam's five pillars (shahada, prayer, zakat, fasting, hajj) give it a remarkably cohesive public practice for a religion with 1.9 billion adherents across 50+ majority-Muslim nations. Explore the main branches:</p>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="islam-btns">
        <button onclick="showBranch('islam','sunni',this)" class="active">Sunni</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('islam','shia',this)">Shia</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('islam','sufi',this)">Sufism</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('islam','ibadi',this)">Ibadi</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('islam','ahmadi',this)">Ahmadiyya</button>
      </div>
      <div class="branch-panel" id="islam-panel"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="svg-wrap">
      <p class="svg-title">Abrahamic family tree and divergence points</p>
      <svg class="religions-media" viewBox="0 0 680 280" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <style is:inline>.abt-text{font-family:var(--font-sans);fill:var(--color-text-primary)}.abt-sub{font-family:var(--font-sans);fill:var(--color-text-secondary)}.abt-line{stroke:var(--color-border-secondary);stroke-width:1.5;fill:none}</style>
