        <text x="110" y="134" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Torah, c.1300 BCE onward</text>
        <rect x="260" y="100" width="160" height="44" rx="8" fill="#27500a" opacity="0.13" stroke="#27500a" stroke-width="1"/>
        <text x="340" y="118" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-text" font-size="12" font-weight="500">Christianity</text>
        <text x="340" y="134" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Jesus of Nazareth, c.30 CE</text>
        <rect x="490" y="100" width="160" height="44" rx="8" fill="#085041" opacity="0.13" stroke="#085041" stroke-width="1"/>
        <text x="570" y="118" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-text" font-size="12" font-weight="500">Islam</text>
        <text x="570" y="134" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Muhammad, 610 CE</text>
        <line x1="80" y1="144" x2="60" y2="188" class="abt-line"/><line x1="110" y1="144" x2="110" y2="188" class="abt-line"/><line x1="140" y1="144" x2="155" y2="188" class="abt-line"/>
        <rect x="20" y="188" width="78" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="59" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Orthodox</text><text x="59" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">strict halakha</text>
        <rect x="71" y="188" width="78" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="110" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Conservative</text><text x="110" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">adaptive</text>
        <rect x="120" y="188" width="68" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="154" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Reform</text><text x="154" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">liberal</text>
        <line x1="300" y1="144" x2="260" y2="188" class="abt-line"/><line x1="340" y1="144" x2="340" y2="188" class="abt-line"/><line x1="380" y1="144" x2="420" y2="188" class="abt-line"/>
        <rect x="215" y="188" width="78" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="254" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Catholic</text><text x="254" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">1.3 billion</text>
        <rect x="302" y="188" width="78" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="341" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Protestant</text><text x="341" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">900 million</text>
        <rect x="388" y="188" width="72" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="424" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Orthodox</text><text x="424" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">260 million</text>
        <line x1="540" y1="144" x2="510" y2="188" class="abt-line"/><line x1="570" y1="144" x2="570" y2="188" class="abt-line"/><line x1="600" y1="144" x2="625" y2="188" class="abt-line"/>
        <rect x="468" y="188" width="72" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="504" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Sunni</text><text x="504" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">85–90%</text>
        <rect x="535" y="188" width="68" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="569" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Shia</text><text x="569" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">10–15%</text>
        <rect x="600" y="188" width="68" height="32" rx="6" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" stroke="var(--color-border-tertiary)" stroke-width="0.5"/><text x="634" y="202" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10">Sufi</text><text x="634" y="215" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="9">mystical</text>
        <text x="340" y="268" text-anchor="middle" class="abt-sub" font-size="10" font-style="italic">All three share: monotheism · Abraham · Jerusalem · prophetic tradition · eschatology</text>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">What the three share — and where they diverge</p>
      <p class="cnote">All three traditions trace their genealogy to Abraham and his covenant with one God; all revere Jerusalem; all are prophetic religions with scriptures that record God's communication with humanity; all anticipate some form of final judgment. The divergence is largely about Jesus and Muhammad. Jews do not accept Jesus as messiah (the messiah has not come yet) and consider Muhammad irrelevant. Christians accept Jesus as divine messiah and son of God but do not accept Muhammad's prophethood. Muslims honour Jesus as a great prophet but reject his divinity — the Quran explicitly states God has no son — and consider Muhammad the final and greatest prophet. The irony is that the closer two traditions are theologically, the sharper their historical conflict: Jewish-Christian relations were far more violent across history than, say, Christian-Buddhist ones.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- PART C -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-c-dharmic-religions-traditions-from-south-asia">Part C · Dharmic religions — traditions from South Asia</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Hinduism — the world's oldest living religion</p>
      <p class="cnote">Hinduism is the world's third-largest religion (1.2 billion) and the hardest to define, because it has no single founder, no central authority, no single scripture, and no agreed-upon creed. Scholar Wendy Doniger calls it "the religion that has no name" — the term "Hindu" was coined by Persian and later British outsiders. What most Hindu traditions share: Brahman (the ultimate impersonal reality underlying all existence), Atman (the individual soul identical with Brahman), Maya (the veil of illusion that makes us perceive a world of distinct objects), Karma (the ethical law of cause and effect across lives), and Dharma (right conduct appropriate to one's role and stage of life). The caste system (varna) is embedded in classical Hindu texts, but it has been challenged from within — by the Bhakti movements, by Ambedkar's Dalit Buddhism, and by the Indian constitution.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Buddhism — the path out of suffering</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-md">Buddhism's founding insight — that suffering (dukkha) arises from craving, and craving from the mistaken belief in a permanent self — is one of the most radical claims in religious history. The Buddha (Siddhartha Gautama, c.563–483 BCE) did not claim divine authority but argued that his teaching could be tested through practice. Explore the main schools:</p>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="buddhism-btns">
        <button onclick="showBranch('buddhism','theravada',this)" class="active">Theravada</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('buddhism','mahayana',this)">Mahayana</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('buddhism','vajrayana',this)">Vajrayana</button>
        <button onclick="showBranch('buddhism','zen',this)">Zen</button>
      </div>
      <div class="branch-panel" id="buddhism-panel"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="slider-widget">
      <p class="slider-title">The spectrum of Buddhist meditation practices</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-sm">Drag to explore different meditative orientations, from concentration-based calm (samatha) to insight-based inquiry (vipassana):</p>
      <div class="slider-poles"><span>Pure calm (samatha)</span><span>Pure insight (vipassana)</span></div>
      <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" id="meditation-slider" oninput="updateMeditationSlider(this.value)">
      <div class="slider-output" id="meditation-output"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Jainism — ahimsa and the most rigorous non-violence</p>
      <p class="cnote">With around 4–6 million adherents (almost all in India), Jainism is tiny by count but immense in influence — Gandhi's doctrine of non-violence drew directly on Jain thought. The core commitment is ahimsa: non-harm to any living being, including insects, which is why monks of the Digambara sect sweep the ground before walking. Karma in Jainism is not metaphorical but literal: physical particles that stick to the soul through bad actions, weighing it down. Liberation (moksha) comes through shedding all karma, which requires extreme asceticism. The Digambara ("sky-clad") and Shvetambara ("white-clad") sects differ on whether monks must be naked to truly renounce the world. Jainism has no creator god; the universe is eternal and self-sustaining.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Sikhism — the youngest major world religion</p>
      <p class="cnote">Founded by Guru Nanak (1469–1539) in the Punjab, Sikhism emerged partly in dialogue with both Hinduism and Islam but developed a distinct theology: one formless God (expressed in "Ik Onkar" — One God), the equality of all humans, and service (seva) as a core spiritual practice. The langar — a free community kitchen attached to every Sikh temple — feeds millions daily with no religious qualification required; it is among the world's largest voluntary feeding operations. The Khalsa (the community of initiated Sikhs, founded 1699) is marked by the Five Ks: kesh (uncut hair), kanga (wooden comb), kara (steel bracelet), kachera (cotton undergarment), kirpan (ceremonial steel sword). There are roughly 25–30 million Sikhs worldwide, two-thirds in the Punjab region of India.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- PART D -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-d-east-asian-traditions-taoism-confucianism-shinto">Part D · East Asian traditions — Taoism, Confucianism, Shinto</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Three distinct systems — quick comparison</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-md">East Asian traditions differ fundamentally from Abrahamic and Dharmic ones: they are often less about belief in the supernatural and more about right relationship — with nature (Taoism), with society (Confucianism), or with the ancestral and spiritual forces of the land (Shinto). People commonly practice multiple traditions simultaneously — a Japanese person may have a Shinto wedding and a Buddhist funeral.</p>
      <div class="bar-row"><div class="bl">Taoism</div><div class="bb bb-style"><span class="bv text-green-900">~20M formal adherents, ~400M cultural influence</span></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><div class="bl">Confucianism</div><div class="bb bb-confucianism"><span class="bv bv-confucianism">Shapes ~1.5B in East/SE Asia</span></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><div class="bl">Shinto</div><div class="bb bb-shinto"><span class="bv text-amber-900">~3–4M devout, ~120M cultural in Japan</span></div></div>
      <p class="cnote u-mt-sm">Formal adherent counts for East Asian traditions are nearly meaningless — cultural practice far exceeds explicit identification. 70% of Japanese report no religion by survey, yet 90% participate in Shinto rituals.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">The three East Asian systems — key concepts</p>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="eastasia-btns">
        <button onclick="showGeneric('eastasia','taoism',this)" class="active">Taoism</button>
        <button onclick="showGeneric('eastasia','confucianism',this)">Confucianism</button>
        <button onclick="showGeneric('eastasia','shinto',this)">Shinto</button>
      </div>
      <div class="explorer-panel" id="eastasia-panel"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Three traditions on a spectrum: how much does the individual matter?</p>
      <div class="cscale-wrap">
        <div class="cscale-poles"><span>Society / cosmos primary</span><span>Individual primary</span></div>
        <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Confucianism</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style"></div></div></div>
        <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Shinto</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-shinto"></div></div></div>
        <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Taoism</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-taoism"></div></div></div>
        <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Buddhism</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-buddhism"></div></div></div>
        <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Protestantism</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-protestantism"></div></div></div>
      </div>
      <p class="cnote">Confucianism places the self firmly within a web of obligations (five relationships: ruler-subject, parent-child, husband-wife, elder-younger, friend-friend). Protestant Christianity is probably the most individualistic major religion: Luther's sola fide (faith alone) and the "priesthood of all believers" placed personal conviction at the centre of salvation.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- PART E -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-e-indigenous-ancient-and-new-religious-movements">Part E · indigenous, ancient, and new religious movements</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Indigenous spiritual traditions — animism, shamanism, and the sacred land</p>
      <p class="cnote">Roughly 300 million people worldwide practice Indigenous spiritual traditions, though the category is so diverse that "tradition" is almost misleading. Animism — the belief that animals, plants, rivers, mountains, and other entities have spiritual agency — is probably humanity's oldest religious form; archaeological evidence (grave goods, cave paintings with animal-human hybrids) suggests it goes back at least 100,000 years. Shamanism is the specialist role of mediating between the human community and the spirit world, found across Siberia, the Americas, Central Asia, and many other regions with no historical connection. Common features: oral transmission (no scripture), sacred geography (specific places are spiritually charged), reciprocity with non-human persons, and seasonal ritual cycles. These traditions are not primitive precursors to "real" religion — many are philosophically sophisticated responses to the question of how humans should relate to the living world they depend on.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Ancient polytheisms — approximate periods of cultural dominance</p>
      <div class="pc-wrap">
        <div class="pc-axis" id="polytheism-axis"></div>
        <div id="polytheism-chart"></div>
      </div>
      <p class="cnote">Greek and Roman religion was not primarily about belief — no creed was required. Participation in civic ritual was compulsory, but private theology was free. The mystery religions (Eleusinian mysteries, Mithraism, Orphism) offered personal transformation experiences closer to what modern people expect from religion. They declined for complex reasons: imperial Christianity's coercive power from 380 CE (Theodosius I made Nicene Christianity the Roman state religion), the appeal of universal monotheism over local pantheons, and intellectual critiques from philosophers who had long been sceptical of the Olympians.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">New religious movements — from Mormonism to Scientology</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-md">A "new religious movement" (NRM) is typically defined as a religious group founded after 1800 and operating outside the mainstream of established traditions. The term is preferred over "cult" by scholars because "cult" has become a pejorative with no agreed definition. Click to explore major NRMs:</p>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="nrm-btns">
        <button onclick="showGeneric('nrm','mormonism',this)" class="active">Mormonism</button>
        <button onclick="showGeneric('nrm','jw',this)">Jehovah's Witnesses</button>
        <button onclick="showGeneric('nrm','scientology',this)">Scientology</button>
        <button onclick="showGeneric('nrm','bahai',this)">Bahai</button>
      </div>
      <div class="explorer-panel" id="nrm-panel"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Atheism, agnosticism, and secular humanism</p>
      <p class="cnote">Atheism (no god) and agnosticism (unknowable whether there is a god) are often conflated but conceptually distinct: agnosticism is an epistemic claim about knowledge, atheism is a metaphysical claim about existence. You can be an agnostic atheist (no belief in god, and the question is not answerable) or an agnostic theist (belief in god, but acknowledged as unprovable). "New Atheism" — associated with Dawkins, Hitchens, Harris, and Dennett from roughly 2004 — argued religion is not just mistaken but actively harmful and deserves no special social deference. Secular humanism is the positive programme: basing ethics on human flourishing and reason without any supernatural grounding. About 1.2 billion people self-identify as non-religious (2024), but surveys consistently find that a large portion of "nones" still hold supernatural beliefs — in fate, spiritual forces, or life after death — making them "believing without belonging" rather than committed materialists.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="calc-widget">
