    <!-- PART F -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-f-religion-in-the-modern-world">Part F · religion in the modern world</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Secularisation — is religion declining?</p>
      <p class="cnote">The secularisation thesis — formulated by sociologists like Bryan Wilson and Peter Berger in the 1960s — predicted that as societies modernise and science explains more, religion would wither. The data since then has forced major revisions. Western Europe did secularise dramatically: the UK went from 72% Christian (1983) to 46% (2018) in one generation, and in Sweden fewer than 5% attend weekly services. But globally, religion is growing: sub-Saharan Africa is on track to have the world's largest Christian population by 2050; Pentecostalism grew from 1 million adherents in 1906 to over 700 million today. Berger himself reversed his position in the 1990s, concluding that the U.S. — extremely religious despite being highly modern — showed secularisation was not inevitable. The better question may not be "is religion declining?" but "what kinds of religion flourish under which conditions?"</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Religion and politics — theocracy, nationalism, and the secular state</p>
      <p class="cnote u-mb-md">The relationship between religious authority and political power has been one of history's great recurring tensions. Explore contemporary configurations:</p>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="relpol-btns">
        <button onclick="showGeneric('relpol','separation',this)" class="active">Church-state separation</button>
        <button onclick="showGeneric('relpol','christian_nationalism',this)">Christian nationalism</button>
        <button onclick="showGeneric('relpol','political_islam',this)">Political Islam</button>
        <button onclick="showGeneric('relpol','hindu_nationalism',this)">Hindu nationalism</button>
      </div>
      <div class="explorer-panel" id="relpol-panel"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Religious violence — a complex relationship</p>
      <p class="cnote">The claim that religion is the primary cause of human conflict is historically dubious — most large-scale violence (World War I, II, the Mongol conquests, the Thirty Years' War at its core) was primarily political, dynastic, or nationalist, with religious framing added for legitimacy. William Cavanaugh's <em>The Myth of Religious Violence</em> (2009) argues the very category of "religious violence" is ideological: it exempts secular nationalism (the 20th century's primary killing ideology) from scrutiny while pathologising non-Western traditions. That said, religion clearly can amplify violence: it provides transcendent motivation, dehumanises the other (heretic, infidel), and promises reward to those who die fighting. The same mechanisms that make religion a powerful force for social cohesion — strong in-group identity, sacred authority, willingness to sacrifice — can be redirected toward violence when a leadership chooses that path.</p>
    </div>

