    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">Religion and science — conflict, independence, or dialogue?</p>
      <p class="cnote">The "conflict thesis" — that religion and science are inherently at war — was popularised by Draper (1874) and White (1896), but historians of science now consider it largely a myth created for 19th-century polemical purposes. The Galileo affair was not primarily about science vs. religion: Galileo was condemned partly because he insulted Pope Urban VIII (a former friend) and because his heliocentrism contradicted the reigning Aristotelian physics, not only Genesis. Isaac Newton, Gregor Mendel (genetics), Georges Lemaitre (Big Bang), and Francisco Varela (cognitive science) were all devout religious believers. Stephen Jay Gould's "Non-Overlapping Magisteria" (NOMA) proposed that science covers fact and theory while religion covers meaning and value — but this fails because most religions do make factual claims (the resurrection, the creation). The most philosophically sophisticated position is probably ongoing dialogue: both religion and science are attempts to understand reality, and both have something to learn from their disagreements.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="slider-widget">
      <p class="slider-title">The future of religion: where do you place yourself on the spectrum?</p>
      <div class="slider-poles"><span>Religion will fade with modernity</span><span>Religion will persist and adapt</span></div>
      <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" id="future-slider" oninput="updateFutureSlider(this.value)">
      <div class="slider-output" id="future-output"></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card u-mb-md">
      <p class="clabel">The future of religion — AI, biotechnology, and meaning</p>
      <p class="cnote">Yuval Harari's "Dataism" thesis suggests that as we delegate more decision-making to algorithms and as neuroscience explains our inner experience, the human soul — the theological concept that grounds most religious ethics — becomes harder to sustain. If there is no free will (a live scientific claim), no permanent self (also defensible), and no dualism of matter and spirit (neuroscience's working assumption), what happens to religious meaning? At the same time, AI and biotech create precisely the questions religion has always addressed. Who counts as a person? What should we do with the power to modify our own consciousness, lifespan, and genome? What do we owe beings we create? These are not scientific questions — they require ethical frameworks, and the major religious traditions have 2,500 or more years of thought about analogous questions. The most likely outcome is not religion's disappearance but its transformation: away from institutional authority and doctrinal uniformity, toward personal meaning-making and ethical community, perhaps increasingly in dialogue with technological change.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- PART G — Q&A -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-g-q-and-a">Part G · Q&A</h2>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">Is Buddhism a religion if it has no god?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Theravada Buddhism is explicitly non-theistic: the Buddha never claimed to be a god or to have created the world, and the core teaching — that suffering arises from craving and can be ended through the Eightfold Path — requires no supernatural being. Yet Buddhism has monks, rituals, sacred texts, temples, pilgrimage sites, and answers ultimate questions about life and death. Under substantive definitions (requires God or supernatural) it barely qualifies; under functional definitions (binds community, addresses ultimate meaning) it qualifies easily. Most scholars today accept Buddhism as a religion, which is a good reason to prefer functional or polythetic definitions — ones that allow gradations rather than hard in/out cuts.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">What is Pascal Boyer's "minimally counter-intuitive" concept?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Boyer (2001) observed that supernatural beings in world religions are almost never totally alien — they are mostly like humans or animals but with one or two impossible properties violated. A ghost is like a person but passes through walls. A god is like a powerful person but knows everything. An ancestor spirit is like a living person but is dead. This "minimally counter-intuitive" structure makes them cognitively sticky: one violation surprises and grabs attention, but otherwise familiar properties mean we can reason about the being using normal social-cognition tools. Beings that are too weird (violating ten intuitive categories at once) do not spread because they are too hard to remember or reason about.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">Are "spiritual but not religious" people actually less religious than traditional believers?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Not necessarily by any cognitive measure. Sociologist Robert Wuthnow distinguished "dwelling" spirituality (anchored in stable institutions) from "seeking" spirituality (personal, eclectic, exploratory). People in the second category may hold just as many supernatural beliefs — in angels, karma, energy fields, cosmic purpose — as churchgoers; they just do not attend services. In the U.S., roughly 27% identify as "spiritual but not religious" (Pew 2017), and many of them hold more supernatural beliefs than moderate churchgoers. The shift is partly from institutional loyalty to consumer spirituality, not from belief to non-belief.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">What is Sharia, and does it mean what Western media usually implies?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Sharia (literally "the path") is Islamic law derived from the Quran, the hadith (reported sayings and actions of Muhammad), and centuries of jurisprudential reasoning (fiqh). It covers not just criminal punishments — the hadd penalties like amputation that dominate Western coverage — but far more: prayer times, dietary rules, financial ethics, family law, commercial contracts, and ritual practice. The criminal elements that Western media fixate on apply in only a small minority of countries, and even there are typically applied to a narrow set of cases. Most Muslims in most countries use Sharia primarily as a personal ethical and ritual guide, not as a state criminal code. The four main Sunni legal schools (Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'i, Hanbali) disagree substantially on specifics, so "Sharia says X" is often an oversimplification.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">Did the Protestant Reformation really change everything?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Yes, but not immediately and not only in religion. Luther's key theological move was sola scriptura ("scripture alone") — the claim that individual Christians could read the Bible themselves and did not need the Church hierarchy to mediate between them and God. This had enormous downstream effects: it spurred mass literacy (people needed to read the Bible), weakened centralised authority, and created dozens of competing denominations within decades. Sociologist Max Weber controversially argued it also created the psychological conditions for capitalism: Calvinist "predestination" theology (you cannot earn salvation) paradoxically made believers work harder in their worldly "calling" as a sign of their likely elect status. That specific claim is disputed, but the broader point — that the Reformation restructured not just theology but politics, economics, and culture — is solid.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">Is Judaism more an ethnicity or a religion?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Both, and that is not confusion — it is a genuine feature of the tradition. Halakha (Jewish law) defines a Jew as someone born of a Jewish mother (or converted), making it partly genealogical. Yet there are atheist Jews who maintain Jewish identity through culture, language, and practice; and there are converts who are fully Jewish without any genetic connection to historical Israelite populations. The "ethnoreligion" category is unusual globally — most major world religions actively seek converts and define membership purely by belief and practice. Judaism's dual nature reflects its origin as the religion of a specific tribal confederation in ancient Canaan; diaspora and persecution reinforced ethnic solidarity even as theological belief varied enormously.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">What exactly is karma, and is it the same across traditions?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">No — karma differs significantly across traditions despite the shared word. In Hinduism, karma (literally "action") is the moral law of cause and effect operating across lifetimes: good actions produce good future births, bad actions produce bad ones. In Buddhism, karma operates similarly but feeds into rebirth without a permanent self doing the re-being reborn — a subtler and philosophically puzzling claim. In Jainism, karma is quasi-physical: actual particles that literally cling to the soul. In popular Western usage, "karma" usually just means "what goes around comes around" — a vague ethical reciprocity with no necessary afterlife component. The common thread is moral causation, but the metaphysical machinery differs completely.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">What is wu wei, and is it really about doing nothing?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Wu wei (無為) is usually translated "non-action" or "effortless action," but the point is not passivity — it is acting in harmony with the Tao (the natural pattern of the universe) rather than forcing, contriving, or going against the grain. The Tao Te Ching's water metaphor is apt: water does not force anything, it finds the natural path, wears away stone through effortless persistence, and reaches the lowest places that the prideful avoid. In practice, wu wei describes the master craftsman who works effortlessly because their skill is perfectly calibrated to the material, or the leader who governs so lightly that people think they governed themselves. It is closest to the concept of "flow" in modern psychology — a state of optimal, effortless performance.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">Was State Shinto actually a religion?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">State Shinto (1868–1945) was the Meiji government's project of turning Shinto from a diverse set of local practices into a centralised national ideology focused on imperial divinity. The government simultaneously claimed it was not a religion (so it did not violate religious freedom for non-Shintoists) while making shrine visits mandatory and propagating the belief that Emperor Hirohito was a living god descended from the sun goddess Amaterasu. This was functional religion: it created sacred narratives, sacred places, compulsory rituals, and claims about ultimate reality. The Shinto Directive of 1945, imposed by the American occupation after Japan's defeat, formally separated state from shrine and required Hirohito to publicly deny his divinity in a 1946 radio broadcast.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">Why did the ancient polytheisms decline?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">No single cause suffices. Imperial Christianity used legal coercion (Theodosius I banned pagan sacrifice in 391 CE) and sometimes violence. But the appeal of monotheism also mattered: a single universal God was philosophically cleaner than a quarrelsome pantheon of limited, morally questionable gods — the same critique Greek philosophers had been making for centuries. The mystery religions that competed with early Christianity were already moving in a more personal, universal direction. Finally, the Roman empire's political collapse took with it the civic structures that maintained traditional religion — many Roman gods were tied to specific civic functions, and when those institutions dissolved, the religion they underwrote dissolved too.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">What distinguishes an NRM from a cult?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Scholars avoid "cult" because it is applied inconsistently and usually just means "a religion I disapprove of." Early Christianity was called a cult (superstitio) by Romans. What scholars do identify is "high-control groups" — organisations that use thought-control techniques (loaded language, information restriction, confession as surveillance, punishment for doubt) to maintain member compliance. These can be found in fringe movements and in established religions. The key warning signs are not doctrinal novelty but behavioural control: does the group restrict members' contact with family and friends? Does it claim exclusive truth and demand unquestioning loyalty to a living leader? Does it punish or shun people who leave? Those features, not theological weirdness, mark genuinely harmful groups.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">Does religion cause more harm than good?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">This question has a lot of empirical content that gets drowned by ideology on both sides. On the positive side: religious communities produce vast amounts of charitable work (the Catholic Church alone runs roughly 26% of the world's healthcare facilities); strong religious participation correlates with lower depression, longer life, higher charitable giving, and stronger social trust in most studies. On the negative side: religious institutions have repeatedly covered up abuse, supported slavery and colonialism, persecuted minorities, and retarded civil rights. The honest answer is that religion is a social technology that amplifies whatever the surrounding community values — it can intensify generosity or persecution with equal efficiency. The key variable is not religiosity per se but whether the tradition's interpretive framework is inclusive or exclusionary, whether leadership is accountable, and whether members maintain contact with outside perspectives.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">Is the Galileo story as simple as "religion vs. science"?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">No — the reality is considerably more complex. Galileo had strong support within the Catholic Church for decades; his Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems (1632) was approved by Church censors. He got into trouble primarily because the book's "Simplicio" (the character who defends the geocentric position) was widely read as mocking Pope Urban VIII, who had been Galileo's patron. The Church's objection was also partly Aristotelian-scientific: Galileo's heliocentrism lacked a mechanism explaining why we do not feel the Earth's motion (that mechanism, inertia, would not come until Newton). He was placed under house arrest (not tortured or imprisoned) and continued his scientific work. The story became a founding myth of secular science vs. religious obscurantism in the 19th century — a useful weapon in conflicts that had nothing to do with Galileo himself.</div></div>

    <div class="qa"><p class="qa-q">What is Christian nationalism, and is it new?</p><button class="qa-btn" onclick="toggleQA(this)">show answer</button><div class="qa-ans">Christian nationalism is the claim that America (or another nation) was founded as, and should remain, a Christian nation — that Christian identity and national identity are essentially fused. It is distinct from ordinary religiosity: surveys show that Christian nationalists are identified more by their political and cultural views than their church attendance or personal devotion. Historically, it is not new — American civil religion (Robert Bellah's term) has always mixed national mythology with Christian symbolism. What is new is its political mobilisation as an explicit programme: movements like the New Apostolic Reformation actively seek to place Christians in key governmental roles to bring society into alignment with biblical law. Scholars like Andrew Whitehead and Samuel Perry (2020) identify roughly 20% of Americans as strong Christian nationalists and another 30% as sympathisers.</div></div>

  

  
  
