    var csrData = {
      agent: { title: 'Hyperactive Agent Detection Device (HADD)', body: "Evolutionary psychologist Justin Barrett argues humans have a 'Hyperactive Agent Detection Device' — a cognitive system that errs on the side of detecting agents (predators, enemies, allies) even where there are none. In our ancestral environment it was better to mistake a shadow for a tiger than to miss a real tiger. The cost of false positives is low; the cost of false negatives is death. This system misfires to produce attributions of agency to wind, rivers, and natural disasters — a plausible route to belief in invisible agents (spirits, gods) acting in the world.", tags: ['Cognitive bias', 'Evolutionary psychology', 'Justin Barrett', 'Agent attribution'] },
      coalition: { title: 'Coalitional psychology and "Big Gods"', body: "Ara Norenzayan's 'Big Gods' hypothesis (2013) argues that belief in a morally concerned, watchful supernatural agent expands the scale of cooperation possible in human societies. Small hunter-gatherer societies police free-riders face-to-face. Large agricultural societies needed an 'imaginary policeman' — a god who monitors everyone and punishes cheating — to solve the cooperation problem. This explains why moralising gods are more common in large, complex societies: they co-evolved with them. It also explains why the phrase 'someone is watching' makes people more honest even when no one literally is.", tags: ['Ara Norenzayan', 'Big Gods', 'Cooperation', 'Moral monitoring'] },
      tmt: { title: 'Terror Management Theory', body: "Psychologists Greenberg, Solomon, and Pyszczynski (1986, building on Ernest Becker's 1973 'Denial of Death') argue that religion is primarily motivated by our awareness of our own mortality — the uniquely human terror of knowing we will die. Cultural worldviews (including religions) give life cosmic significance that transcends individual death, making death less paralysing. Experimental evidence: reminding people of their mortality (death salience) reliably increases defence of their cultural worldview, hostility to those with different worldviews, and religiosity. Terror management is one of the more empirically tested theories in social psychology.", tags: ['Terror management', 'Mortality salience', 'Ernest Becker', 'Death anxiety'] },
      boyer: { title: 'Pascal Boyer — minimally counter-intuitive concepts', body: "Pascal Boyer (2001, 'Religion Explained') argues that supernatural beings spread culturally because they are 'minimally counter-intuitive' — they violate just one or two intuitive domain properties (physics, biology, psychology) while conforming to all others. A ghost is like a person but passes through walls. This makes them attention-grabbing (one violation) and easy to reason about (the normal person-schema applies to everything else). Concepts that violate too many properties are too hard to remember; ordinary concepts are too boring to remember and pass on. Minimal counter-intuitiveness is the cognitive sweet spot for cultural transmission.", tags: ['Pascal Boyer', 'Cognitive science', 'Minimally counter-intuitive', 'Cultural transmission'] }
    };
