    var genericData = {
      eastasia: {
        taoism: { title: 'Taoism — the Way of naturalness', body: "The Tao (道, 'the Way') is the ultimate principle underlying reality — not a personal god but the pattern according to which all things spontaneously arise, exist, and pass away. The Tao Te Ching, attributed to Laozi (perhaps 6th century BCE, though historicity is uncertain), conveys its teaching through paradox: 'The Tao that can be named is not the eternal Tao.' Wu wei (non-action) means acting in harmony with this pattern rather than forcing. Zhuangzi's more playful texts extend the teaching with brilliant fables — the cook who butchers an ox so perfectly that his knife never dulls, finding the natural spaces in the animal. Religious Taoism (as opposed to philosophical Taoism) is a tradition of temples, liturgy, immortality practices, and a complex pantheon quite different from the spare philosophy of the Tao Te Ching.", tags: ['Tao Te Ching', 'Wu wei', 'Laozi', 'Philosophical vs religious'] },
        confucianism: { title: 'Confucianism — social ethics as cosmic order', body: "Confucius (551–479 BCE) was not primarily a religious figure but a political reformer who believed that if rulers and subjects performed their roles correctly — embodying ren (benevolence), li (ritual propriety), and yi (righteousness) — social harmony would naturally follow. The Five Relationships (ruler-subject, parent-child, husband-wife, elder-younger, friend-friend) define a person's obligations. Neo-Confucianism (Song dynasty, 10th–13th century CE) added metaphysical depth: the li (principle) inherent in all things, the cultivation of the self through reflection. Confucianism is not obviously a religion by substantive definitions (no god, no afterlife emphasis) but functions as one: it provides a framework of ultimate meaning and ethical orientation. Its influence on East Asian governance, education, and family structure is arguably deeper than any other tradition.", tags: ['Ren', 'Li', 'Five relationships', 'Neo-Confucianism'] },
        shinto: { title: "Shinto — Japan's indigenous tradition", body: "Shinto has no founder, no canonical scripture, and no clear theology — it is more a practice tradition than a doctrinal one. Its central concept is kami: spiritual forces or presences that inhabit natural places (mountains, rivers, particular trees), ancestors, and exceptional phenomena. Interaction with kami is through ritual purity, shrine visits, and seasonal festivals (matsuri). The coexistence of Shinto and Buddhism is Japan's religious norm: most Japanese have Shinto weddings and Buddhist funerals, participate in both traditions without tension, and self-report as having 'no religion' when surveyed. State Shinto (1868–1945) weaponised this tradition for imperial nationalism, requiring school visits to shrines and teaching the Emperor's divine descent.", tags: ['Kami', 'Ritual purity', 'State Shinto', 'Matsuri'] }
      },
      nrm: {
        mormonism: { title: 'Mormonism (Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints)', body: "Founded 1830 by Joseph Smith in upstate New York, the LDS church claims Smith received golden plates containing a new scripture (the Book of Mormon) narrating a pre-Columbian history of Christ's ministry in the Americas. Membership: roughly 17 million worldwide. Core distinctive beliefs: God has a physical body; humans can become gods; the church is the restored original church of Christ; baptism of the dead allows deceased ancestors to retroactively accept Mormon theology. Mormon missionary culture (two-year missions for young adults) accounts for rapid growth. The church is headquartered in Salt Lake City and is by some measures one of the wealthiest religious organisations per capita in the world.", tags: ['Joseph Smith', 'Book of Mormon', '17M members', 'Restoration theology'] },
        jw: { title: "Jehovah's Witnesses", body: "Founded in the 1870s by Charles Taze Russell in Pennsylvania, JWs reject the Trinity (Jesus is a created being, not God), military service, blood transfusions, and many national symbols (flag saluting, national anthems). The organisation is controlled by a central 'Governing Body.' They famously predicted the end of the world in 1914, 1918, 1925, and 1975 — a pattern of failed prophecy that scholars use to study how groups handle cognitive dissonance (most members reinterpreted rather than left). About 8.7 million members; their door-to-door preaching makes them probably the most publicly visible NRM in Western countries.", tags: ['Charles Russell', 'Governing Body', '8.7M members', 'Failed prophecy'] },
        scientology: { title: 'Scientology', body: "Founded 1954 by science fiction writer L. Ron Hubbard, Scientology is built around 'auditing' — a form of counselling using an 'E-meter' (galvanic skin response device) to locate and release 'engrams' (traumatic mental residues). Advanced levels (OT levels) reveal cosmological doctrines about Xenu, an alien dictator who deposited souls (thetans) on Earth. Membership estimates range from 20,000 to 100,000. Its legal battles with the IRS — resolved in 1993 when it won tax-exempt status as a religion — are studied as a case in how the law defines religion. Critics document systematic harassment of defectors; the organisation denies these accounts.", tags: ['L. Ron Hubbard', 'Auditing', 'OT levels', 'Tax status controversy'] },
        bahai: { title: 'Bahai Faith', body: "Founded 1844 in Persia by the Bab and developed by Baha'u'llah, the Bahai Faith is perhaps the most theologically inclusive NRM: it holds that all major prophets — Abraham, Krishna, Moses, Zoroaster, Buddha, Jesus, Muhammad — were manifestations of the same divine reality appropriate to their historical moment, and that Baha'u'llah is the most recent manifestation. About 5–8 million members in nearly every country. Core principles: unity of humanity, gender equality, harmony of religion and science, universal education, world government. In Iran (its birthplace) Bahais face severe persecution — considered apostates from Islam — making it one of the most persecuted religious minorities today.", tags: ["Baha'u'llah", 'Progressive revelation', '5–8M members', 'Persecution in Iran'] }
      },
      relpol: {
        separation: { title: 'Church-state separation — the liberal ideal', body: "The strict separation of church and state, enshrined in the U.S. First Amendment ('Congress shall make no law respecting an establishment of religion') and the French concept of laicite, holds that religious and political authority operate in completely separate spheres. France's laicite is the most aggressive version: it bans religious symbols in public schools and government spaces and requires that all public reasoning be 'secular' in character. The American version is more permissive: religious language in politics is acceptable, but state endorsement of specific religions is not. Philosopher John Rawls argued that in a pluralistic society, political arguments in the public sphere should be made in terms all citizens can accept — not in terms that require acceptance of a particular religious premise.", tags: ['First Amendment', 'Laicite', 'John Rawls', 'Pluralism'] },
        christian_nationalism: { title: 'Christian nationalism in the United States', body: "Christian nationalism holds that America was founded as a Christian nation and that its laws, institutions, and culture should reflect Christian values. Scholars Andrew Whitehead and Samuel Perry (2020) distinguish it from ordinary religiosity: Christian nationalists are more likely to support authoritarian leadership, racial hierarchy, and the view that 'real Americans' are Christians. The New Apostolic Reformation (NAR) is a network of charismatic Christian leaders pursuing 'Seven Mountain Dominionism' — the explicit goal of Christians occupying the 'seven mountains' of society: government, business, education, media, arts, family, and religion. As of 2020 surveys, roughly 20% of Americans are strong Christian nationalists, with another 30% as sympathisers.", tags: ['Dominionism', 'NAR', 'Whitehead and Perry', 'Seven Mountains'] },
        political_islam: { title: 'Political Islam (Islamism)', body: "Islamism — the project of organising states and societies around Islamic law — is primarily a 20th-century phenomenon, emerging in reaction to colonial disruption and secular nationalism. Hassan al-Banna founded the Muslim Brotherhood in Egypt in 1928; Sayyid Qutb (executed 1966) radicalised its ideology, arguing that contemporary Muslim societies were in a state of jahiliyya (pre-Islamic ignorance) requiring revolutionary change. The 1979 Iranian Revolution produced the first modern Islamist state. Today Islamism encompasses a spectrum: the AKP in Turkey (electoral Islamism), the Muslim Brotherhood (gradualist), Hamas (resistance nationalism), and ISIS (millenarian violence). Polls consistently show majorities in Muslim countries support democracy and reject ISIS.", tags: ['Muslim Brotherhood', 'Sayyid Qutb', 'Iranian Revolution', 'Spectrum of Islamism'] },
        hindu_nationalism: { title: 'Hindu nationalism (Hindutva)', body: "Hindutva ('Hindu-ness'), articulated by V.D. Savarkar in 1923, holds that Indian identity is fundamentally Hindu and that Muslims and Christians are foreigners to the Hindu rashtra (nation). The RSS (Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh), founded 1925, is the ideological core of a wider movement (sangh parivar). The BJP (Bharatiya Janata Party), in government under Narendra Modi, is the political arm. Hindu nationalism has been implicated in anti-Muslim violence (the 2002 Gujarat riots occurred under Modi's governorship) and the erosion of India's secular constitution. Critics argue it represents a fundamental revision of India's founding pluralism; supporters argue it defends Hinduism against historical Muslim and colonial oppression.", tags: ['Savarkar', 'RSS', 'BJP', 'Modi', 'Hindutva'] }
      }
